\section{Precision and size}{

Each encoder comes in up to three precisions. Measured on dimoose's 55
example photos against the PyTorch model (CLIP ViT-B/32 image encoder):\tabular{lll}{
   \code{precision} \tab image encoder \tab cosine with PyTorch (lowest) \cr
   \code{"fp32"} \tab 352 MB \tab 1.0000 \cr
   \code{"fp16"} \tab 176 MB \tab 1.0000 \cr
   \code{"int8"}, converted by \code{\link[=exportOnnxModel]{exportOnnxModel()}} \tab 96 MB \tab 0.998 \cr
   \code{"int8"}, \code{Xenova/clip-vit-base-patch32} (\verb{_quantized}) \tab 89 MB \tab 0.81 \cr
}


\code{"fp16"} halves the download but runs up to three times slower on a CPU
than \code{"fp32"}. BioCLIP converted by \code{\link[=exportOnnxModel]{exportOnnxModel()}} is 345, 173 and
89 MB, with cosines of 1.0000, 1.0000 and 0.998 (BioCLIP 2, a larger
model, is about 1217, 609 and 310 MB); on the Montana example photos all
three precisions name the same species. A key must be followed with the
model \strong{and precision} it was built with when the precision is \code{"int8"}:
its embeddings differ slightly from the others'. The ready-made \code{"int8"}
file of \code{Xenova/clip-vit-base-patch32} is a much rougher copy (0.89 on
average), so \code{onnxModel()} warns when it is used; to get the close one,
convert the model yourself with \code{exportOnnxModel("ViT-B-32")}. A model you
have converted is used in place of the ready-made copy of the same name.

The patch tokens of an \code{"int8"} encoder (\code{embedImages(tiles = 0)}) are
less faithful than its image embeddings: with BioCLIP the lowest cosine
of a token with the \code{"fp32"} one was 0.86. Use tiles, or a higher
precision, to find sniglets with patch tokens.
}

\section{Layouts of a model folder}{

dimoose's own layout, written by \code{\link[=exportOnnxModel]{exportOnnxModel()}}: \code{dimoose_onnx.json}
(architecture, image size, preprocessing constants, file names),
\code{image_encoder.onnx}, \code{text_encoder.onnx}, their \verb{_fp16} and \verb{_int8}
copies, and \code{tokenizer.json}. Its image encoder also returns the model's
patch tokens, so \code{embedImages(tiles = 0)} works.

The transformers.js layout of ready-made exports on the Hub: \code{config.json},
\code{preprocessor_config.json}, \code{tokenizer.json},
\verb{onnx/vision_model[_fp16|_quantized].onnx} and
\verb{onnx/text_model[_fp16|_quantized].onnx}. These give one embedding per
image and no patch tokens: tiles (the default of \code{\link[=embedImages]{embedImages()}}) work,
\code{tiles = 0} does not.
}

\section{When something goes wrong}{

\itemize{
\item \emph{Could not download ...}: the model is fetched from huggingface.co the
first time only. Files that arrived are kept, so calling again continues
where it stopped. A computer that cannot reach the Hub can be given the
model's folder, copied from \code{\link[=onnxCache]{onnxCache()}} on one that can, as \code{name}.
\item \emph{... is not a readable ONNX model}: a model file is damaged, usually a
download or copy that stopped early. Delete the file named in the
message and load the model again; a file from the Hub is fetched afresh.
\item \emph{Could not load dimoose's ONNX code}: the Python in use lacks
\code{onnxruntime} or \code{tokenizers}. The message gives the
\code{reticulate::py_install()} call that installs them.
}
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
